[
  {
    "id": "hicFindTADs.h5.small_test_matrix.fdr",
    "tool": "hicFindTADs",
    "tier": 5,
    "args": ["-m", "{data}/small_test_matrix.h5",
             "--minDepth", "60000", "--maxDepth", "180000", "--step", "20000",
             "--minBoundaryDistance", "20000",
             "--correctForMultipleTesting", "fdr", "--thresholdComparisons", "0.1",
             "--outPrefix", "{out}/t"],
    "outputs": [
      {"path": "{out}/t_tad_score.bm", "format": "text", "class": "E0"},
      {"path": "{out}/t_score.bedgraph", "format": "text", "class": "E0"},
      {"path": "{out}/t_boundaries.bed", "format": "text", "class": "E0"},
      {"path": "{out}/t_boundaries.gff", "format": "text", "class": "E0"},
      {"path": "{out}/t_domains.bed", "format": "text", "class": "E0"},
      {"path": "{out}/t_zscore_matrix.h5", "format": "h5", "class": "E2"}
    ],
    "threads_arg": "--numberOfProcessors",
    "memory": {"budget_mb": 184},
    "notes": "The case the Python suite covers. Both text outputs and the z-score matrix are byte identical, which is far stronger than the ED gate; the reference calls only 2 boundaries and 0 domains here, so the .bm and the bedgraph are what actually constrain the run. The budget is not the alpha * W of PLAN.md 4.5: this tool's working set is not the input matrix but the z-score band, nbins * int(maxDepth * 3.75 / binsize) entries at 12 bytes each, which is 54.7 MB here against a 0.7 MB input. budget_mb is 2.2 * that + 64 MB. See the note in the report; PLAN.md risk 7 asks for exactly this revision."
  },
